course: dictionary
lesson: 9410
title: Squashy
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Squashy describes something soft that gives way when you press it. Look at the word: it is squash with a y on the end. «Maddie and Lily became best friends on the first day of Reception, because they both cried and the teacher sat them on the same squashy beanbag.» A beanbag that is squashy is soft and cosy, so the clue is that they sat in a comfy seat.
explain: Squashy can also describe fruit that is too soft and past its best. Look at the clues around the word. If the thing is comfy, squashy means soft in a good way. If it is spoiled, squashy means too soft. Try your meaning in the sentence to check that it still makes sense.
explain: Squashy is an informal word, mainly used in Britain. Scientists also use it to describe materials like sponge, marshmallow and foam, which change shape when pressed and may spring back.
fact: Squashy means soft and easy to press or squeeze out of shape.
fact: Squashy fruit is too soft and a bit squashed, often because it is overripe.
fact: The comparative and superlative forms are squashier and squashiest.
fact: «That is why bike helmets have squashy foam, gymnasts land on mats and cars have airbags.» Soft materials help to protect us when we fall or crash.
fact: Squashy is an informal word, mainly used in Britain.
exercise: «Maddie and Lily sat on the squashy beanbag.» What does squashy mean here? | Hard and cold like a rock | Very loud | Wet from the rain | *Soft and easy to press
exercise: A banana left on the table for a week is squashy. What does squashy mean? | Sharp and pointy | Very hard and cold | Fresh and crunchy | *Too soft and past its best
exercise: Which word from «That is why bike helmets have squashy foam, gymnasts land on mats and cars have airbags.» tells you the foam is soft? | helmets | *squashy | gymnasts | airbags
test: Which word is closest in meaning to squashy? | Sharp | Hard | Cold | *Spongy
test: Is squashy a formal word used in every country? | Yes, only scientists use it | No, it means very fast | *No, it is an informal word, mainly used in Britain | Yes, it is used in every country in formal writing
exam: Which sentence uses squashy correctly to mean too soft? | The bananas flew across the room. | The bananas were loud and noisy. | The bananas were crisp and crunchy. | *The bananas were squashy and brown, past their best.
